<section class="panel" aria-label="Independently authored subtraction representation tasks"><h2>Subtract Your Way — Grade 3 Unit 3 Lesson 7</h2><p>Flexible subtraction within 1,000 using pupils' existing place-value strategies. Use base-ten blocks or a number line. A teacher reviews strategies, original graphics and discussion.</p><h3>Warm-up: Number Talk: Subtract Two-Digit Numbers (10 minutes)</h3><p>Source-context mathematical checks (write your reasoning separately):</p><ol><li>50 - 10</li><li>58 - 10</li><li>258 - 20</li><li>258 - 24</li></ol><p>Discuss patterns or make a mental number talk before checking.</p><h3>Activity 1: Strategies to Subtract (25 minutes)</h3><p>Source-context mathematical checks (write your reasoning separately):</p><ol><li>428 - 213</li><li>505 - 398</li><li>394 - 127</li></ol><p>Use a strategy or algorithm that fits, then justify steps.</p><h3>Activity 2: Base-ten Drawings (10 minutes)</h3><p>Source-context mathematical checks (write your reasoning separately):</p><ol><li>262 - 135</li><li>252 - 181</li><li>252 - 132</li></ol><p>Compare source drawings or written records with a partner; the teacher checks the match.</p><div role="img" aria-label="First-party place value drawing: 262 regrouped as two hundreds five tens twelve ones; subtract 135 equals 127" style="border:1px solid #8daeb7;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><h3>How can 262 − 135 be represented?</h3><p>262 starts as 2 hundreds, 6 tens and 2 ones. Exchange one ten for ten ones: 2 hundreds, 5 tens and 12 ones. Subtract 1 hundred, 3 tens and 5 ones. Remaining: 1 hundred, 2 tens, 7 ones = 127.</p><p>Jada may draw individual blocks; Han may mark changes on a ten. Show how both representations preserve the quantities, with teacher guidance. This is not a source-image copy.</p></div><h3>Visuals and explanation — teacher review</h3><ul><li>Compare a base-ten drawing that unbundles ten individual ones with one that marks ten partitions directly on the ten; explain why both show 262−135.</li><li>Match each original publisher base-ten diagram with the correct expression; the original image pixels are not present.</li><li>Create a visual display with a partner for one subtraction, then compare different representations in a gallery discussion (MLR7).</li><li>Rehearse and explain with a partner how the diagrams are alike and different (MLR8).</li></ul><h3>Method and access</h3><p>Start with four mental subtractions. For 428−213 use hundreds, tens and ones to get 215. For 505−398 use counting on from 398 to 505 to get 107, or decompose across a zero. For 394−127 the difference is 267. After pairs construct visual displays, compare number lines, equations and base-ten blocks (MLR7). Activity 2 contrasts Jada and Han: Jada exchanges a ten for ten ones and draws each one; Han partitions and marks a ten without physically redrawing ten individual units. Both depict 262−135=127. Match source diagrams for 252−181=71,262−135=127,252−132=120; image association and pair explanations require teacher review with MLR8. The source calls for actual base-ten blocks and tools for a visual display.</p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-3/lesson-7/lesson.html">2021 official public teacher lesson</a> · <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-3/lesson-7/preparation.html">Official preparation and Student Task Statements link</a>. Source PDF/illustration parity is unverified. Original gated Cool-down is NOT reproduced.</p><p><a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/8x37i4q7p7lahfk53tx2928n8khw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original 2021 four-page Student Task Statements PDF</a></p></section>
